ia">Time<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>zone</a></th><td class="infobox-data"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC+10:00" title="UTC+10:00">UTC+10:00</a> (AEST)</td></tr><tr class="mergedrow"><th scope="row" class="infobox-label"><span class="nowrap"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>•<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Summer (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylight_saving_time" title="Daylight saving time">DST</a>)</span></th><td class="infobox-data"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC+11:00" title="UTC+11:00">UTC+11:00</a> (AEDT)</td></tr><tr class="mergedtoprow"><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Mean max temp</th><td class="infobox-data">20.4<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°C (68.7<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°F)</td></tr><tr class="mergedrow"><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Mean min temp</th><td class="infobox-data">7.0<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°C (44.6<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°F)</td></tr><tr class="mergedrow"><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Annual rainfall</th><td class="infobox-data">579.5<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>mm (22.81<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>in)</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p id="mwNA"><b id="mwNQ">Canberra</b> (<span class="rt-commentedText nowrap" about="#mwt66" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwNg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"IPAc-en","href":"./Template:IPAc-en"},"params":{"audio":{"wt":"En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg"},"1":{"wt":"ˈ"},"2":{"wt":"k"},"3":{"wt":"æ"},"4":{"wt":"n"},"5":{"wt":"b"},"6":{"wt":"r"},"7":{"wt":"ə"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class="IPA nopopups noexcerpt" lang="en-fonipa"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English" title="Help:IPA/English">/<span style="border-bottom:1px dotted"><span title="/ˈ/: primary stress follows">ˈ</span><span title="'k' in 'kind'">k</span><span title="/æ/: 'a' in 'bad'">æ</span><span title="'n' in 'nigh'">n</span><span title="'b' in 'buy'">b</span><span title="'r' in 'rye'">r</span><span title="/ə/: 'a' in 'about'">ə</span></span>/</a></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span><span class="ext-phonos" typeof="mw:Extension/phonos" about="#mwt67" data-mw='{"name":"phonos","attrs":{"file":"En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'><span data-nosnippet="" id="ooui-php-1" class="noexcerpt ext-phonos-PhonosButton ext-phonos-PhonosButton-emptylabel oo-ui-widget oo-ui-widget-enabled oo-ui-buttonElement oo-ui-buttonElement-frameless oo-ui-buttonElement-size-medium oo-ui-iconElement oo-ui-buttonWidget" data-ooui='{"_":"mw.Phonos.PhonosButton","href":"\/\/upload.wikimedia.org\/wikipedia\/commons\/transcoded\/2\/21\/En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg\/En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg.mp3","rel":["nofollow"],"framed":false,"icon":"volumeUp","data":{"ipa":"","text":"","lang":"en","wikibase":"","file":"En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg"},"classes":["noexcerpt","ext-phonos-PhonosButton","ext-phonos-PhonosButton-emptylabel"]}'><a role="button" tabindex="0" href="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/21/En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg/En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg.mp3" rel="nofollow" aria-label="Play audio" title="Play audio" class="oo-ui-buttonElement-button"><span class="oo-ui-iconElement-icon oo-ui-icon-volumeUp"></span><span class="oo-ui-labelElement-label"></span><span class="oo-ui-indicatorElement-indicator oo-ui-indicatorElement-noIndicator"></span></a></span><sup class="ext-phonos-attribution noexcerpt navigation-not-searchable"><a href="/wiki/File:En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg" title="File:En-au-Canberra-ACT.ogg">ⓘ</a></sup></span></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_including_recorded_pronunciations" about="#mwt66" id="mwNw"/> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Pronunciation_respelling_key" title="Help:Pronunciation respelling key" about="#mwt68" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwOA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Respell","href":"./Template:Respell"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"KAN"},"2":{"wt":"brə"}},"i":0}}]}'><i title="English pronunciation respelling"><span style="font-size:90%">KAN</span>-brə</i></a>; <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngunawal_language" title="Ngunawal language" about="#mwt69" typeof="mw:Transclusion" class="mw-redirect" id="mwOQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Langx","href":"./Template:Langx"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"xul"},"2":{"wt":"Kanbarra"}},"i":0}}]}'>Ngunawal</a><span about="#mwt69">: </span><i lang="xul" about="#mwt69">Kanbarra</i><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Ngunawal-language_text" about="#mwt69" id="mwOg"/>) is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_city" title="Capital city" id="mwOw">capital city</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australia" title="Australia" id="mwPA">Australia</a> and the largest population centre in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Australian Capital Territory" id="mwPQ">Australian Capital Territory</a>. With an estimated population of 484,630 as of 2025, Canberra is Australia's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_cities_in_Australia" title="List of cities in Australia" id="mwPg">largest inland city</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_cities_in_Australia_by_population" title="List of cities in Australia by population" id="mwPw">eighth-largest Australian city by population</a>. The city occupies most of the northern quadrant of the Australian Capital Territory, and its urban sprawl borders the state of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_South_Wales" title="New South Wales" id="mwQA">New South Wales</a> to the northwest, northeast and southeast. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brindabella_Range" title="Brindabella Range" id="mwQQ">Brindabellas</a> are located south and west of the city, and form the northern part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Alps" title="Australian Alps" id="mwQg">Australian Alps</a>, the country's highest mountain range.</p>

<p id="mwQw">Canberra was established following the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federation_of_Australia" title="Federation of Australia" id="mwRA">federation of the Australian colonies</a> to serve as the seat of government for the new nation, being a compromise between <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sydney" title="Sydney" id="mwRQ">Sydney</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melbourne" title="Melbourne" id="mwRg">Melbourne</a>, Australia’s two largest cities. The area chosen for the capital had been inhabited by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aboriginal_Australians" title="Aboriginal Australians" id="mwRw">Aboriginal Australians</a> for up to 21,000 years, by groups including the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngunnawal" title="Ngunnawal" id="mwSA">Ngunnawal</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngambri" title="Ngambri" id="mwSQ">Ngambri</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Australia_(1788–1850)" title="History of Australia (1788–1850)" id="mwSg">European settlement</a> commenced in the first half of the 19th century, evidenced by surviving landmarks such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_John_the_Baptist_Church,_Reid" title="St John the Baptist Church, Reid" id="mwSw">St John's Anglican Church</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blundells_Cottage" title="Blundells Cottage" id="mwTA">Blundells Cottage</a>. On 1 January 1901, federation of the colonies of Australia was achieved. The capital city was founded and formally named as Canberra in 1913. Unusual among Australian cities, it is an entirely <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planned_community" title="Planned community" id="mwTQ">planned city</a>, grounded in a design by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States" title="United States" id="mwTg">American</a> architects <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Burley_Griffin" title="Walter Burley Griffin" id="mwTw">Walter Burley Griffin</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marion_Mahony_Griffin" title="Marion Mahony Griffin" id="mwUA">Marion Mahony Griffin</a>. The Griffins' plan was influenced by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_city_movement" title="Garden city movement" id="mwUQ">garden city movement</a> and featured geometric motifs aligned with significant topographical landmarks such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Mountain_(Australian_Capital_Territory)" title="Black Mountain (Australian Capital Territory)" id="mwUg">Black Mountain</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Ainslie" title="Mount Ainslie" id="mwUw">Mount Ainslie</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_Hill,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Capital Hill, Australian Capital Territory" id="mwVA">Capital Hill</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Hill,_Canberra" title="City Hill, Canberra" id="mwVQ">City Hill</a>. Its design can be viewed from its highest point at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telstra_Tower" title="Telstra Tower" id="mwVg">Telstra Tower</a> and the summit of Mount Ainslie. Other notable features include the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Arboretum_Canberra" title="National Arboretum Canberra" id="mwVw">National Arboretum</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Burley_Griffin" title="Lake Burley Griffin" id="mwWA">Lake Burley Griffin</a>.</p>

<p id="mwWQ">As the seat of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_Australia" title="Government of Australia" class="mw-redirect" id="mwWg">Government of Australia</a>, Canberra is home to many important institutions of the federal government, national monuments and museums. These include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwWw">Parliament House</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_House,_Canberra" title="Government House, Canberra" id="mwXA">Government House</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Court_of_Australia_Building" title="High Court of Australia Building" id="mwXQ">High Court building</a> and the headquarters of numerous government agencies. Social and cultural institutions of national significance include the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_War_Memorial" title="Australian War Memorial" id="mwXg">Australian War Memorial</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_National_University" title="Australian National University" id="mwXw">Australian National University</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Australian_Mint" title="Royal Australian Mint" id="mwYA">Royal Australian Mint</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Institute_of_Sport" title="Australian Institute of Sport" id="mwYQ">Australian Institute of Sport</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Gallery_of_Australia" title="National Gallery of Australia" id="mwYg">National Gallery</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Museum_of_Australia" title="National Museum of Australia" id="mwYw">National Museum</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Library_of_Australia" title="National Library of Australia" id="mwZA">National Library</a>. The city is home to many important institutions of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Defence_Force" title="Australian Defence Force" id="mwZQ">Australian Defence Force</a> including the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Military_College,_Duntroon" title="Royal Military College, Duntroon" id="mwZg">Royal Military College Duntroon</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Defence_Force_Academy" title="Australian Defence Force Academy" id="mwZw">Australian Defence Force Academy</a>. It hosts <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_diplomatic_missions_in_Australia#Diplomatic_missions_in_Canberra" title="List of diplomatic missions in Australia" id="mwaA">all foreign embassies</a> in Australia as well as regional headquarters of many international organisations, not-for-profit groups, lobbying groups and professional associations.</p>

<p id="mwaQ">Canberra has been ranked among the world's best cities to live in and visit. Compared to the national averages, the unemployment rate is lower and the average income higher; tertiary education levels are higher, while the population is younger. At the 2021 Census, 28.7% of Canberra's inhabitants were reported as having been born overseas. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Public_Service" title="Australian Public Service" id="mwag">Australian Public Service</a> accounted for about 25% all jobs as at November 2025. Other major industries include health care, professional services, education and training, retail, accommodation and food, and construction. Annual cultural events include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floriade_(Canberra)" title="Floriade (Canberra)" id="mwaw">Floriade</a>, the largest flower festival in the Southern Hemisphere, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enlighten_Canberra" title="Enlighten Canberra" id="mwbA">Enlighten Festival</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyfire_(Canberra)" title="Skyfire (Canberra)" id="mwbQ">Skyfire</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Multicultural_Festival" title="National Multicultural Festival" id="mwbg">National Multicultural Festival</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summernats" title="Summernats" id="mwbw">Summernats</a>. Canberra's main sporting venues are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Stadium" title="Canberra Stadium" id="mwcA">Canberra Stadium</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuka_Oval" title="Manuka Oval" id="mwcQ">Manuka Oval</a>. The city is served with domestic and international flights at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Airport" title="Canberra Airport" id="mwcg">Canberra Airport</a>, while interstate train and coach services depart from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_railway_station" title="Canberra railway station" id="mwcw">Canberra railway station</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jolimont_Centre" title="Jolimont Centre" id="mwdA">Jolimont Centre</a> respectively. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Interchange" title="City Interchange" id="mwdQ">City Interchange</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alinga_Street_light_rail_station" title="Alinga Street light rail station" id="mwdg">Alinga Street station</a> form the hub of Canberra's bus and light rail transport network.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwdw" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mweA" aria-labelledby="Name"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Name">Name</h2></div>
<p id="mweQ">The word "Canberra" is derived from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngunnawal_language" title="Ngunnawal language" class="mw-redirect" id="mweg">Ngunnawal language</a> of a local <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngunnawal" title="Ngunnawal" id="mwew">Ngunnawal</a> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngambri" title="Ngambri" id="mwfA">Ngambri</a> clan who resided in the area and were referred to by the early British colonists as either the <span title="Ngunawal-language text" about="#mwt70" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwfQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"xul"},"2":{"wt":"Canberry"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="xul">Canberry</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Ngunawal-language_text" about="#mwt70" id="mwfg"/>, <span title="Ngunawal-language text" about="#mwt71" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwfw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"xul"},"2":{"wt":"Kanberri"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="xul">Kanberri</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Ngunawal-language_text" about="#mwt71" id="mwgA"/> or <span title="Ngunawal-language text" about="#mwt72" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwgQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"xul"},"2":{"wt":"Nganbra"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="xul">Nganbra</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Ngunawal-language_text" about="#mwt72" id="mwgg"/> tribe.<sup about="#mwt75" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-9" id="mwgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt78" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:9_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":9"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:9-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:9-10" id="mwhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiQ">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwig">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt81" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-11" id="mwiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjQ">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjg">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_John_Moore" title="Joshua John Moore" id="mwjw">Joshua John Moore</a>, the first European land-owner in the region, named his grant "Canberry" in 1823 after these people. "Canberry Creek" and "Canberry" first appeared on regional maps from 1830, while the derivative name "Canberra" started to appear from around 1857.<sup about="#mwt84" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-selkirk_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"selkirk"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-selkirk-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-selkirk-12" id="mwkA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt87" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-13" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-13" id="mwlA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwlQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlg">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt90" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-14" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-14" id="mwmA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmg">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmw">]</span></span></a></sup> Other early recorded variants of the spelling include "Canbury" (potentially influenced by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canbury" title="Canbury" id="mwnA">the settlement of the same name in England</a>), "Canburry" and "Kembery".<sup about="#mwt93" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-15" id="mwnQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwng"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnw">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwoA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwoQ">Numerous local commentators, including the Ngunnawal elder Don Bell, have speculated upon possible meanings of "Canberra" over the years. These include "meeting place", "woman's breasts" and "the hollow between a woman's breasts".<sup about="#mwt96" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Frei_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Frei"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Frei-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Frei-16" id="mwog"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwow"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpA">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt99" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-17" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-17" id="mwpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqA">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">]</span></span></a></sup> References to breasts or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleavage_(breasts)" title="Cleavage (breasts)" id="mwqg">the space between them</a> are thought to derive from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Mountain_(Australian_Capital_Territory)" title="Black Mountain (Australian Capital Territory)" id="mwqw">Black Mountain</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Ainslie" title="Mount Ainslie" id="mwrA">Mount Ainslie</a>, two large hills with similar elevations situated immediately to the northwest and northeast, respectively, of what is now <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civic,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Civic, Australian Capital Territory" id="mwrQ">the city centre</a>.<sup about="#mwt102" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-18" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-18" id="mwrg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwrw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsA">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt105" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwsg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtA">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt108" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-20" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-20" id="mwtg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuA">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwug">Alternative proposals for the name of the city during its planning included Austral, Australville, Aurora, Captain Cook, Caucus City, Cookaburra, Dampier, Eden, Eucalypta, Flinders, Gonebroke, Home, Hopetoun, Kangaremu, Myola, Meladneyperbane, New Era, Olympus, Paradise, Shakespeare, Sydmelperadbrisho, Swindleville, The National City, Union City, Unison, Wattleton, Wheatwoolgold, Yass-Canberra.<sup about="#mwt111" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-21" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-21" id="mwuw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwvA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvQ">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt114" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-22" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-22" id="mwvw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwwA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwQ">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt117" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-23" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-23"}}'><a href="#cite_note-23" id="mwww"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxQ">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwxw" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt118" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwyA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"History of Canberra"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt119" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt118" id="mwyQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Canberra" title="History of Canberra">History of Canberra</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt120" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwyg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"History of the Australian Capital Territory"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt121" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt120" id="mwyw">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="History of the Australian Capital Territory">History of the Australian Capital Territory</a></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwzA" aria-labelledby="First_Nations_peoples"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="First_Nations_peoples">First Nations peoples</h3></div>
<p id="mwzQ">The first peoples of the Canberra area include the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngunnawal" title="Ngunnawal" id="mwzg">Ngunnawal</a>, Ngunawal and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngambri" title="Ngambri" id="mwzw">Ngambri</a> peoples.<sup about="#mwt124" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:8_24-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":8"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:8-24"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:8-24" id="mw0A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw0Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0g">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0w">]</span></span></a></sup> Other groups claiming a connection to the land include the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngarigo" title="Ngarigo" id="mw1A">Ngarigo</a> (who also lived directly to the south) and the Ngambri-Guumaal.<sup about="#mwt125" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:9_10-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:9-10" id="mw1Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw1g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw1w">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2A">]</span></span></a></sup> Neighbouring groups include the Wandandian to the east, the Walgulu also to the south, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gandangara_people" title="Gandangara people" class="mw-redirect" id="mw2Q">Gandangara people</a> to the north and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiradjuri" title="Wiradjuri" id="mw2g">Wiradjuri</a> to the north-west.</p>

<p id="mw2w">The first British settlers into the Canberra area described two clans of Ngunnawal people resident to the vicinity. The <i id="mw3A">Canberry</i> or <i id="mw3Q">Nganbra</i> clan lived mostly around Sullivan's Creek and had ceremonial grounds at the base of Galambary (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Mountain_(Australian_Capital_Territory)" title="Black Mountain (Australian Capital Territory)" id="mw3g">Black Mountain</a>), while the <i id="mw3w">Pialligo</i> clan had land around what is now <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Airport" title="Canberra Airport" id="mw4A">Canberra Airport</a>.<sup about="#mwt128" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bluett_25-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bluett"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bluett-25"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bluett-25" id="mw4Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4w">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt131" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-26" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-26"}}'><a href="#cite_note-26" id="mw5Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw5g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5w">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6A">]</span></span></a></sup> The people living here carefully managed and cultivated the land with fire, farmed yams, and hunted for food.<sup about="#mwt134" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:7_27-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":7"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:7-27"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:7-27" id="mw6Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6w">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw7Q">Archaeological evidence of settlement in the region includes inhabited rock shelters, rock paintings and engravings, burial places, camps and quarry sites as well as stone tools and arrangements.<sup about="#mwt137" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Gillespie84_28-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Gillespie84"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Gillespie84-28"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Gillespie84-28" id="mw7g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw7w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8A">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8Q">]</span></span></a></sup> Artefacts suggests early human activity occurred at some point in the area 21,000 years previously.<sup about="#mwt140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_29-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:1-29"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:1-29" id="mw8g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9A">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw9g">Still today, Ngunnawal men into the present conduct ceremony on the banks of the river, Murrumbidgee River. They travel upstream as they receive their Totems and corresponding responsibilities for land management. 'Murrum' means 'Pathway' and Bidgee means 'Boss'.<sup about="#mwt141" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:7_27-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:7-27" id="mw9w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw-A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-Q">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw-w">The submerged limestone caves beneath Lake Burley Griffin contained Aboriginal rock art, some of the only sites in the region.<sup about="#mwt142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:7_27-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:7-27" id="mw_A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw_Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_g">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAQA">Galambary (Black Mountain) is an important Aboriginal meeting and business site, predominantly for men's business. According to the Ngunnawal and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngambri" title="Ngambri" id="mwAQE">Ngambri</a> people, Mt Ainslie is primarily for place of women's business. Black Mountain and Mount Ainslie are referred to as women's breasts. Galambary was also used by Ngunnawal people as an initiation site, with the mountain itself said to represent the growth of a boy into a man.<sup about="#mwt143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:7_27-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:7-27" id="mwAQI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQQ">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwAQY" aria-labelledby="British_exploration_and_colonisation"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="British_exploration_and_colonisation">British exploration and colonisation</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAQc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAQg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg/250px-St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg/500px-St_johns_church_in_reid_canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1136" data-file-height="880" data-file-type="bitmap" height="194" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAQk"/></a><figcaption id="mwAQo"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_John_the_Baptist_Church,_Reid" title="St John the Baptist Church, Reid" id="mwAQs">St John's Anglican Church</a>, the oldest surviving public building in the inner city, consecrated in 1845</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAQw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAQ0"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg/330px-Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg/960px-Blundells_Cottage_overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4000" data-file-height="2252" data-file-type="bitmap" height="163" width="290" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.15" id="mwAQ4"/></a><figcaption id="mwAQ8"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blundells_Cottage" title="Blundells Cottage" id="mwARA">Blundells Cottage</a>, built around 1860,<sup about="#mwt162" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BLUNDELL_30-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BLUNDELL"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BLUNDELL-30"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BLUNDELL-30" id="mwARE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARM">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARQ">]</span></span></a></sup> is one of the few remaining buildings built by the first white settlers of Canberra.</figcaption></figure><p id="mwARU"> In October 1820, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Throsby_Smith" title="Charles Throsby Smith" id="mwARY">Charles Throsby Smith</a> led the first British expedition to the area.<sup about="#mwt152" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cambage_31-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cambage"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-cambage-31"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cambage-31" id="mwARc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARk">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt155" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-32" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-32"}}'><a href="#cite_note-32" id="mwARs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR0">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt144" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19875_33-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19875"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19875-33"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"5"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19875-33" id="mwAR8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASE">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt147" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie19913–8_34-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie19913–8"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie19913–8-34"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"pp":{"wt":"3-8"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie19913–8-34" id="mwASM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASU">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASY">]</span></span></a></sup> Four other expeditions occurred between 1820 and 1823 with the first accurate map being produced by explorer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_John_Currie" title="Mark John Currie" id="mwASc">Mark John Currie</a> in June 1823. By this stage, the area had become known as the Limestone Plains.<sup about="#mwt156" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cambage_31-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cambage"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cambage-31" id="mwASg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASo">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt159" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-35" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-35"}}'><a href="#cite_note-35" id="mwASw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAS0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS4">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwATA">British settlement of the area probably dates from late 1823, when a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheep_station" title="Sheep station" id="mwATE">sheep station</a> was formed on what is now the Acton Peninsula by James Cowan, the head stockman employed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joshua_John_Moore" title="Joshua John Moore" id="mwATI">Joshua John Moore</a>.<sup about="#mwt163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie19919_36-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie19919"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie19919-36"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"p":{"wt":"9"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie19919-36" id="mwATM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATU">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATY">]</span></span></a></sup> Moore had received a land grant in the region in 1823 and formally applied to purchase the site on 16 December 1826. He named the property "Canberry". On 30 April 1827, Moore was told by letter that he could retain possession of <span about="#mwt166" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwATc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1000"},"2":{"wt":"acres"},"3":{"wt":"0"}},"i":0}}]}'>1,000 acres (405</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt166"> </span><span about="#mwt166">ha)</span> at Canberry.<sup about="#mwt169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-37" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-37"}}'><a href="#cite_note-37" id="mwATg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATo">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwATw">Other colonists soon followed Moore's example to take up land in the region. Around 1825, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Ainslie_(pastoralist)" title="James Ainslie (pastoralist)" id="mwAT0">James Ainslie</a>, working on behalf of the wealthy merchant <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Campbell_(1769–1846)" title="Robert Campbell (1769–1846)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAT4">Robert Campbell</a>, arrived to establish a sheep station. He was guided to the region by a local Aboriginal girl who showed him the fine lands of her <i id="mwAT8">Pialligo</i> clan.<sup about="#mwt179" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bluett_25-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bluett"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bluett-25" id="mwAUA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUI">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUM">]</span></span></a></sup> The area then became the property of Campbell and it was initially named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pialligo,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Pialligo, Australian Capital Territory" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAUQ">Pialligo</a> before Campbell changed it to the Scottish title of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duntroon,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Duntroon, Australian Capital Territory" id="mwAUU">Duntroon</a>.<sup about="#mwt180" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-selkirk_12-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"selkirk"}}'><a href="#cite_note-selkirk-12" id="mwAUY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt170" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney198848_38-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney198848"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney198848-38"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"48"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney198848-38" id="mwAUo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUw">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt173" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19879_39-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19879"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19879-39"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"9"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald19879-39" id="mwAU4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAU8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVA">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVE">]</span></span></a></sup> Campbell and his family built a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dairy" title="Dairy" id="mwAVI">dairy</a> on the site in 1832, now regarded as the oldest standing European building in Canberra,<sup about="#mwt183" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-40" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-40"}}'><a href="#cite_note-40" id="mwAVM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVU">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt186" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-41" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-41"}}'><a href="#cite_note-41" id="mwAVc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVk">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt189" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-42" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-42"}}'><a href="#cite_note-42" id="mwAVs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAV0">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAV4">]</span></span></a></sup> followed by the imposing stone house that is now the officers' mess of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Military_College,_Duntroon" title="Royal Military College, Duntroon" id="mwAV8">Royal Military College, Duntroon</a>.<sup about="#mwt192" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-43" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-43"}}'><a href="#cite_note-43" id="mwAWA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWI">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWM">]</span></span></a></sup> The Campbells sponsored settlement by other farmer families to work their land, such as the Southwells of "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weetangera,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Weetangera, Australian Capital Territory" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAWQ">Weetangera</a>".<sup about="#mwt176" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney198887–95_44-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney198887–95"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney198887–95-44"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"87-95"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney198887–95-44" id="mwAWU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWc">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAWk">Other notable early colonists included Henry Donnison, who established the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarralumla,_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Yarralumla, Australian Capital Territory" id="mwAWo">Yarralumla</a> estate—now the site of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_House,_Canberra" title="Government House, Canberra" id="mwAWs">official residence</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor-General" title="Governor-General" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAWw">Governor-General</a> of Australia—in 1827, and John Palmer who employed Duncan Macfarlane to form the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerrabomberra_(district)" title="Jerrabomberra (district)" id="mwAW0">Jerrabomberra</a> property in 1828. A year later, John MacPherson established the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springbank_Island" title="Springbank Island" id="mwAW4">Springbank</a> estate, becoming the first British owner-occupier in the region.<sup about="#mwt193" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-selkirk_12-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"selkirk"}}'><a href="#cite_note-selkirk-12" id="mwAW8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXE">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt196" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-45" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-45"}}'><a href="#cite_note-45" id="mwAXM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXU">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt199" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-46" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-46"}}'><a href="#cite_note-46" id="mwAXc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXk">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAXs">The Anglican church of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_John_the_Baptist_Church,_Reid" title="St John the Baptist Church, Reid" id="mwAXw">St John the Baptist</a>, in the suburb of Reid,<sup about="#mwt200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988116_47-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988116"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988116-47"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"116"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988116-47" id="mwAX0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAX4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX8">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYA">]</span></span></a></sup> was consecrated in 1845, and is now the oldest surviving public building in the city.<sup about="#mwt203" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie199178_48-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie199178"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie199178-48"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"p":{"wt":"78"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie199178-48" id="mwAYE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYM">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt206" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198717_49-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198717"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198717-49"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"17"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198717-49" id="mwAYU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYc">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYg">]</span></span></a></sup> St John's churchyard contains the earliest graves in the district.<sup about="#mwt211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-50" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-50"}}'><a href="#cite_note-50" id="mwAYk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYs">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYw">]</span></span></a></sup> It has been described as a "sanctuary in the city",<sup about="#mwt214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-51" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-51"}}'><a href="#cite_note-51" id="mwAY0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAY4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAY8">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-52" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-52"}}'><a href="#cite_note-52" id="mwAZE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZM">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZQ">]</span></span></a></sup> remaining a small English village-style church even as the capital grew around it. Canberra's first school, St John's School (now a museum), was situated next to the church and opened in the same year of 1845.<sup about="#mwt220" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-53" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-53"}}'><a href="#cite_note-53" id="mwAZU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZc">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZg">]</span></span></a></sup> It was built to educate local settlers children,<sup about="#mwt223" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Nohra_54-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Nohra"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Nohra-54"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Nohra-54" id="mwAZk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZs">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZw">]</span></span></a></sup> including the Blundell children who lived in nearby <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blundell's_Cottage" title="Blundell's Cottage" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAZ0">Blundell's Cottage</a>.<sup about="#mwt226" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sue_W_55-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sue W"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sue_W-55"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Sue_W-55" id="mwAZ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaA">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAaI">As the European presence increased, the Indigenous population dwindled largely due to the destruction of their society, dislocation from their lands and from introduced diseases such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Influenza" title="Influenza" id="mwAaM">influenza</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smallpox" title="Smallpox" id="mwAaQ">smallpox</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcoholism" title="Alcoholism" id="mwAaU">alcoholism</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Measles" title="Measles" id="mwAaY">measles</a>.<sup about="#mwt229" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-56" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href="#cite_note-56" id="mwAac"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAag"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAak">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAao">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt230" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-canb_57-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"canb"}}'><a href="#cite_note-canb-57" id="mwAas"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAaw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAa0">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAa4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwAa8" aria-labelledby="Creation_of_the_nation's_capital"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Creation_of_the_nation's_capital"><span id="Creation_of_the_nation.27s_capital" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Creation of the nation's capital</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAbA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Parliamenthouse2.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAbE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Parliamenthouse2.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Parliamenthouse2.jpg/330px-Parliamenthouse2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Parliamenthouse2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x" data-file-width="453" data-file-height="177" data-file-type="bitmap" height="113" width="290" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.15" id="mwAbI"/></a><figcaption id="mwAbM">The opening of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Old Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAbQ">Parliament House</a> in May 1927</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAbU">The district's change from a rural area in New South Wales to the national capital started during debates over <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federation_of_Australia" title="Federation of Australia" id="mwAbY">federation</a> in the late 19th century.<sup about="#mwt231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792_58-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"92"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58" id="mwAbc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbk">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991220–230_59-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie1991220–230"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991220–230-59"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"pp":{"wt":"220-230"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991220–230-59" id="mwAbs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb0">[</span>59<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb4">]</span></span></a></sup> Following a long dispute over whether Sydney or Melbourne should be the national capital,<sup about="#mwt255" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:2_60-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:2-60"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:2-60" id="mwAb8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcE">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcI">]</span></span></a></sup> a compromise was reached: the new capital would be built in New South Wales, so long as it was at least <span about="#mwt237" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAcM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"100"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"},"4":{"wt":"-1"}},"i":0}}]}'>100</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt237"> </span><span about="#mwt237">mi (160</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt237"> </span><span about="#mwt237">km)</span> from Sydney,<sup about="#mwt238" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792_58-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"92"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58" id="mwAcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcY">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcc">]</span></span></a></sup> with Melbourne to be the temporary seat of government while the new capital was built.<sup about="#mwt241" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore197124_61-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore197124"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197124-61"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"p":{"wt":"24"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197124-61" id="mwAcg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAck"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAco">[</span>61<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcs">]</span></span></a></sup> A survey was conducted across several sites in New South Wales with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombala" title="Bombala" id="mwAcw">Bombala</a>, southern <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monaro_(New_South_Wales)" title="Monaro (New South Wales)" id="mwAc0">Monaro</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange,_New_South_Wales" title="Orange, New South Wales" id="mwAc4">Orange</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yass,_New_South_Wales" title="Yass, New South Wales" id="mwAc8">Yass</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albury" title="Albury" id="mwAdA">Albury</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamworth,_New_South_Wales" title="Tamworth, New South Wales" id="mwAdE">Tamworth</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armidale" title="Armidale" id="mwAdI">Armidale</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tumut" title="Tumut" id="mwAdM">Tumut</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalgety,_New_South_Wales" title="Dalgety, New South Wales" id="mwAdQ">Dalgety</a> all discussed.<sup about="#mwt258" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_62-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:0-62"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-62" id="mwAdU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdc">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdg">]</span></span></a></sup> Dalgety was chosen by the federal parliament and it passed the <i id="mwAdk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seat_of_Government_Act_1904" title="Seat of Government Act 1904" id="mwAdo">Seat of Government Act 1904</a></i> confirming Dalgety as the site of the nation's capital. However, the New South Wales government refused to cede the required territory as they did not accept the site.<sup about="#mwt259" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_62-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-62" id="mwAds"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd0">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd4">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1906, the New South Wales Government finally agreed to cede sufficient land provided that it was in the Yass-Canberra region as this site was closer to Sydney.<sup about="#mwt244" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792_58-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"92"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58" id="mwAd8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeE">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeI">]</span></span></a></sup> Newspaper proprietor <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Gale_(journalist)" title="John Gale (journalist)" id="mwAeM">John Gale</a> circulated a pamphlet titled 'Dalgety or Canberra: Which?' advocating Canberra to every member of the Commonwealth's seven state and federal parliaments. By many accounts, it was decisive in the selection of Canberra as the site in 1908 as was a result of survey work done by the government <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surveying" title="Surveying" id="mwAeQ">surveyor</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Scrivener" title="Charles Scrivener" id="mwAeU">Charles Scrivener</a>.<sup about="#mwt247" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198793_63-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198793"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198793-63"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"93"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198793-63" id="mwAeY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAec"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeg">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAek">]</span></span></a></sup> The NSW government ceded the district to the federal government in 1911 and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Australian Capital Territory" id="mwAeo">Federal Capital Territory</a> was established.<sup about="#mwt250" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792_58-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"92"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald198792-58" id="mwAes"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAew"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe0">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAe8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Sketch_of_canberra.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAfA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Sketch_of_canberra.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sketch_of_canberra.jpg/250px-Sketch_of_canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sketch_of_canberra.jpg/500px-Sketch_of_canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="1067" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAfE"/></a><figcaption id="mwAfI">The Griffins' plan for Canberra</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAfM">An international design competition was launched by the Department of Home Affairs on 30 April 1911, closing on 31 January 1912. The competition was boycotted by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Institute_of_British_Architects" title="Royal Institute of British Architects" id="mwAfQ">Royal Institute of British Architects</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institution_of_Civil_Engineers" title="Institution of Civil Engineers" id="mwAfU">Institution of Civil Engineers</a> and their affiliated bodies throughout the British Empire because the Minister for Home Affairs <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_O'Malley" title="King O'Malley" id="mwAfY">King O'Malley</a> insisted that the final decision was for him to make rather than an expert in city planning.<sup about="#mwt272" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tom_64-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tom"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-tom-64"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tom-64" id="mwAfc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfk">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfo">]</span></span></a></sup> A total of 137 valid entries were received. O'Malley appointed a three-member board to advise him but they could not reach unanimity. On 24 May 1911,<sup about="#mwt260" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987100_65-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987100"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987100-65"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"100"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987100-65" id="mwAfs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAf0">[</span>65<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAf4">]</span></span></a></sup> O'Malley came down on the side of the majority of the board with the design by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Burley_Griffin" title="Walter Burley Griffin" id="mwAf8">Walter Burley Griffin</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marion_Mahony_Griffin" title="Marion Mahony Griffin" id="mwAgA">Marion Mahony Griffin</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago" title="Chicago" id="mwAgE">Chicago</a>, Illinois, United States, being declared the winner.<sup about="#mwt263" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991178_66-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie1991178"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991178-66"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"p":{"wt":"178"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991178-66" id="mwAgI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgQ">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt266" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971160–166_67-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore1971160–166"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971160–166-67"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"pp":{"wt":"160-166"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971160–166-67" id="mwAgY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgg">[</span>67<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgk">]</span></span></a></sup> Second was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eliel_Saarinen" title="Eliel Saarinen" id="mwAgo">Eliel Saarinen</a> of Finland and third was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Agache_(architect)" title="Alfred Agache (architect)" id="mwAgs">Alfred Agache</a> of Brazil but resident in Paris, France.<sup about="#mwt273" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tom_64-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tom-64" id="mwAgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAg0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAg4">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAg8">]</span></span></a></sup> O'Malley then appointed a six-member board to advise him on the implementation of the winning design. On 25 November 1912, the board advised that it could not support the Griffins' plan in its entirety and suggested an alternative plan of its own devising. This plan ostensibly incorporated the best features of the three place-getting designs as well as of a fourth design by H. Caswell, R.C.G. Coulter and W. Scott-Griffiths of Sydney, the rights to which it had purchased. It was this composite plan that was endorsed by Parliament and given formal approval by O'Malley on 10 January 1913.<sup about="#mwt274" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tom_64-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tom-64" id="mwAhA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhI">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhM">]</span></span></a></sup> However, it was the Griffin plan which was ultimately proceeded with. In 1913, Walter Burley Griffin was appointed Federal Capital Director of Design and Construction and construction began.<sup about="#mwt269" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore197163_68-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore197163"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197163-68"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"p":{"wt":"63"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197163-68" id="mwAhQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhY">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhc">]</span></span></a></sup> On 23 February, King O'Malley drove the first peg in the construction of the future capital city.</p>

<p id="mwAhg">In 1912, the government invited suggestions from the public as to the name of the future city. Almost 750 names were suggested. At midday on 12<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAhk"> </span>March 1913,<sup about="#mwt275" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991303_69-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGillespie1991303"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991303-69"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gillespie"},"2":{"wt":"1991"},"p":{"wt":"303"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGillespie1991303-69" id="mwAho"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhw">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAh0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-70" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70"}}'><a href="#cite_note-70" id="mwAh4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAh8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiA">[</span>70<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiE">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrude_Denman,_Baroness_Denman" title="Gertrude Denman, Baroness Denman" id="mwAiI">Lady Denman</a>, the wife of Governor-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Denman,_3rd_Baron_Denman" title="Thomas Denman, 3rd Baron Denman" id="mwAiM">Lord Denman</a>, announced that the city would be named "Canberra" at a ceremony at Kurrajong Hill,<sup about="#mwt278" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987103_71-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987103"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987103-71"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"103"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987103-71" id="mwAiQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAiU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiY">[</span>71<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAic">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt281" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAustralian_Bureau_of_Statistics1963_72-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEAustralian Bureau of Statistics1963"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEAustralian_Bureau_of_Statistics1963-72"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Australian Bureau of Statistics"},"2":{"wt":"1963"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAustralian_Bureau_of_Statistics1963-72" id="mwAig"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAik"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAio">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAis">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt305" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-73" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href="#cite_note-73" id="mwAiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAi0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAi4">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAi8">]</span></span></a></sup> which has since become Capital Hill and the site of the present <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAjA">Parliament House</a>.<sup about="#mwt284" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987105_74-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987105"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987105-74"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"105"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987105-74" id="mwAjE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjM">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjQ">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Day" title="Canberra Day" id="mwAjU">Canberra Day</a> is a public holiday observed in the ACT on the second Monday in March to celebrate the founding of Canberra.<sup about="#mwt308" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-canb_57-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"canb"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-canb-57"}}'><a href="#cite_note-canb-57" id="mwAjY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjg">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjk">]</span></span></a></sup> After the ceremony, bureaucratic disputes hindered Griffin's work;<sup about="#mwt287" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore197170–71_75-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore197170–71"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197170–71-75"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"pp":{"wt":"70-71"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197170–71-75" id="mwAjo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjw">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAj0">]</span></span></a></sup> a Royal Commission in 1916 ruled his authority had been usurped by certain officials and his original plan was reinstated.<sup about="#mwt290" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987101_76-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987101"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987101-76"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"101"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987101-76" id="mwAj4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAj8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkA">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkE">]</span></span></a></sup> Griffin's relationship with the Australian authorities was strained and a lack of funding meant that by the time he was fired in<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAkI"> </span>1920, little work had been done.<sup about="#mwt293" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission19884_77-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTENational Capital Development Commission19884"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission19884-77"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"National Capital Development Commission"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"4"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission19884-77" id="mwAkM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkU">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt296" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore197169–79_78-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore197169–79"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197169–79-78"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"pp":{"wt":"69-79"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore197169–79-78" id="mwAkc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkk">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAko">]</span></span></a></sup> By this time, Griffin had revised his plan, overseen the earthworks of major avenues and established the Glenloch Cork Plantation.<sup about="#mwt311" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-act_79-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"act"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-act-79"}}'><a href="#cite_note-act-79" id="mwAks"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAk0">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAk4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt314" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-80" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80"}}'><a href="#cite_note-80" id="mwAk8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlE">[</span>80<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwAlM" aria-labelledby="Development_throughout_20th_century"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Development_throughout_20th_century">Development throughout 20th century</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAlQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_Government_House.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAlU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_Government_House.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Canberra_Government_House.jpg/250px-Canberra_Government_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Canberra_Government_House.jpg/500px-Canberra_Government_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="1067" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAlY"/></a><figcaption id="mwAlc">Canberra's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_House,_Canberra" title="Government House, Canberra" id="mwAlg">Government House</a>, the official residence of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor-General_of_Australia" title="Governor-General of Australia" id="mwAlk">Governor-General of Australia</a></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAlo"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAls"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg/250px-CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg/500px-CSIRO_ScienceImage_11612_Parliament_House_Canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2657" data-file-height="1989" data-file-type="bitmap" height="187" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAlw"/></a><figcaption id="mwAl0">The land-axis aligns <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAl4">Parliament House</a> (foreground) with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Old Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAl8">Old Parliament House</a> (background) </figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAmA">The Commonwealth government purchased the pastoral property of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_House,_Canberra" title="Government House, Canberra" id="mwAmE">Yarralumla</a> in 1913 to provide an official residence for the Governor-General of Australia in the new capital.<sup about="#mwt329" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Australiana_Fund_81-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Australiana Fund"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Australiana_Fund-81"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Australiana_Fund-81" id="mwAmI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmQ">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmU">]</span></span></a></sup> Renovations began in 1925 to enlarge and modernise the property.<sup about="#mwt332" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-82" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82"}}'><a href="#cite_note-82" id="mwAmY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmg">[</span>82<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmk">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1927, the property was officially dubbed Government House.<sup about="#mwt333" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Australiana_Fund_81-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Australiana Fund"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Australiana_Fund-81" id="mwAmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmw">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAm0">]</span></span></a></sup> On 9 May that year, the Commonwealth parliament moved to Canberra with the opening of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Old Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAm4">Provisional Parliament House</a>.<sup about="#mwt315" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987130_83-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987130"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987130-83"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"130"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987130-83" id="mwAm8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnE">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt336" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-84" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84"}}'><a href="#cite_note-84" id="mwAnM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnU">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnY">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_Minister_of_Australia" title="Prime Minister of Australia" id="mwAnc">Prime Minister</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanley_Bruce" title="Stanley Bruce" id="mwAng">Stanley Bruce</a> had officially taken up residence in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lodge_(Australia)" title="The Lodge (Australia)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAnk">The Lodge</a> a few days earlier.<sup about="#mwt339" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-85" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href="#cite_note-85" id="mwAno"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAns"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnw">[</span>85<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt318" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971101_86-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore1971101"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971101-86"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"p":{"wt":"101"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971101-86" id="mwAn4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAn8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoA">[</span>86<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoE">]</span></span></a></sup> Planned development of the city slowed significantly during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression_in_Australia" title="Great Depression in Australia" id="mwAoI">depression</a> of the 1930s and during World War<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAoM"> </span>II.<sup about="#mwt321" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971125–128_87-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore1971125–128"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971125–128-87"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"pp":{"wt":"125-128"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971125–128-87" id="mwAoQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoY">[</span>87<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoc">]</span></span></a></sup> Some projects planned for that time, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Catholicism_in_Australia" title="Roman Catholicism in Australia" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAog">Roman Catholic</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglican_Church_of_Australia" title="Anglican Church of Australia" id="mwAok">Anglican</a> cathedrals, were never completed.<sup about="#mwt324" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988116–126_88-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988116–126"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988116–126-88"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"116-126"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988116–126-88" id="mwAoo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAos"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAow">[</span>88<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAo0">]</span></span></a></sup> (Nevertheless, in 1973 the Roman Catholic parish church of St. Christopher was remodelled into <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Christopher's_Cathedral,_Manuka" title="St Christopher's Cathedral, Manuka" id="mwAo4">St Christopher's Cathedral, Manuka</a>, serving the Archdiocese of Canberra and Goulburn. It is the only cathedral in Canberra.<sup about="#mwt342" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-89" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-89"}}'><a href="#cite_note-89" id="mwAo8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApE">[</span>89<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApI">]</span></span></a></sup>)</p>

<p id="mwApM">From 1920 to 1957, three bodies — successively the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Capital_Advisory_Committee" title="Federal Capital Advisory Committee" class="mw-redirect" id="mwApQ">Federal Capital Advisory Committee</a>,<sup about="#mwt343" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987115_90-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987115"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987115-90"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"115"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987115-90" id="mwApU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApc">[</span>90<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApg">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Capital_Commission" title="Federal Capital Commission" class="mw-redirect" id="mwApk">Federal Capital Commission</a>,<sup about="#mwt346" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987128_91-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987128"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987128-91"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"128"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987128-91" id="mwApo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAps"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApw">[</span>91<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAp0">]</span></span></a></sup> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Capital_Planning_and_Development_Committee" title="National Capital Planning and Development Committee" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAp4">National Capital Planning and Development Committee</a> — continued to plan the further expansion of Canberra in the absence of Griffin. However, they were only advisory and development decisions were made without consulting them, which increased inefficiency.<sup about="#mwt352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-act_79-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"act"}}'><a href="#cite_note-act-79" id="mwAp8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqE">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt349" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971113_92-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore1971113"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971113-92"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"p":{"wt":"113"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971113-92" id="mwAqM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqU">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAqc">The largest event in Canberra up to World War II was the 24th Meeting of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANZAAS" title="ANZAAS" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAqg">ANZAAS</a> in January 1939. <i id="mwAqk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Canberra_Times" title="The Canberra Times" id="mwAqo">The Canberra Times</a></i> described it as "a signal event ... in the history of this, the world's youngest capital city". The city's accommodation was not nearly sufficient to house the 1,250 delegates and a tent city had to be set up on the banks of the Molonglo River. One of the prominent speakers was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H._G._Wells" title="H. G. Wells" id="mwAqs">H. G. Wells</a>, who was a guest of the Governor-General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Hore-Ruthven,_1st_Earl_of_Gowrie" title="Alexander Hore-Ruthven, 1st Earl of Gowrie" id="mwAqw">Lord Gowrie</a> for a week. This event coincided with a heatwave across south-eastern Australia during which the temperature in Canberra reached 108.5 degrees Fahrenheit (42.5 Celsius) on 11 January. On Friday, 13 January, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Friday_bushfires" title="Black Friday bushfires" id="mwAq0">Black Friday bushfires</a> caused 71 deaths in Victoria and Wells accompanied the Governor-General on his tour of areas threatened by fires.<sup about="#mwt353" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-93" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-93"}}'><a href="#cite_note-93" id="mwAq4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAq8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArA">[</span>93<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwArI">Immediately after the end of the war, Canberra was criticised for resembling a village and its disorganised collection of buildings was deemed ugly.<sup about="#mwt354" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke19886_94-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke19886"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19886-94"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"6"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19886-94" id="mwArM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArU">[</span>94<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt357" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke19881–3_95-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke19881–3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19881–3-95"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"1-3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19881–3-95" id="mwArc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArk">[</span>95<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAro">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt360" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke19887–9_96-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke19887–9"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19887–9-96"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"7-9"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19887–9-96" id="mwArs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr0">[</span>96<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr4">]</span></span></a></sup> Canberra was often derisively described as "several suburbs in search of a city".<sup about="#mwt383" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-97" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-97"}}'><a href="#cite_note-97" id="mwAr8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsE">[</span>97<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsI">]</span></span></a></sup> Prime Minister <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Robert_Menzies" title="Sir Robert Menzies" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAsM">Sir Robert Menzies</a> regarded the state of the national capital as an embarrassment.<sup about="#mwt363" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke198830_98-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke198830"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke198830-98"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"30"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke198830-98" id="mwAsQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsY">[</span>98<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsc">]</span></span></a></sup> Over time his attitude changed from one of contempt to that of championing its development. He fired two ministers charged with the development of the city for poor performance. Menzies remained in office for over a decade and in that time the development of the capital sped up rapidly.<sup about="#mwt366" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke198831–32_99-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke198831–32"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke198831–32-99"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"31-32"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke198831–32-99" id="mwAsg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAso">[</span>99<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAss">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt369" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323_100-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104, 145, 188, 323"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323-100"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"103-104, 145, 188, 323"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323-100" id="mwAsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAs0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs4">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs8">]</span></span></a></sup> The population grew by more than 50<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAtA"> </span>per<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAtE"> </span>cent in every five-year period from 1955 to<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAtI"> </span>1975.<sup about="#mwt372" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323_100-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104, 145, 188, 323"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323-100"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"103-104, 145, 188, 323"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988103–104,_145,_188,_323-100" id="mwAtM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtU">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtY">]</span></span></a></sup> Several Government departments, together with public servants, were moved to Canberra from Melbourne following the war.<sup about="#mwt375" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971111–120_101-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWigmore1971111–120"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971111–120-101"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wigmore"},"2":{"wt":"1971"},"pp":{"wt":"111-120"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWigmore1971111–120-101" id="mwAtc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtk">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAto">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_housing_in_the_Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Public housing in the Australian Capital Territory" id="mwAts">Government housing</a> projects were undertaken to accommodate the city's growing population.<sup about="#mwt378" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988230–242_102-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988230–242"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988230–242-102"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"230-242"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988230–242-102" id="mwAtw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAt0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt4">[</span>102<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAuA">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Capital_Development_Commission" title="National Capital Development Commission" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAuE">National Capital Development Commission</a> (NCDC) formed in 1957 with executive powers and ended four decades of disputes over the shape and design of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Burley_Griffin" title="Lake Burley Griffin" id="mwAuI">Lake Burley Griffin</a> — the centrepiece of Griffin's design — and construction was completed in<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAuM"> </span>1964 after four years of work.<sup about="#mwt384" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988130–140_103-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988130–140"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988130–140-103"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"130-140"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988130–140-103" id="mwAuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAuU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuY">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuc">]</span></span></a></sup> The completion of the lake finally laid the platform for the development of Griffin's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliamentary_Triangle,_Canberra" title="Parliamentary Triangle, Canberra" id="mwAug">Parliamentary Triangle</a>.<sup about="#mwt387" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988170–180_104-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988170–180"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988170–180-104"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"170-180"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988170–180-104" id="mwAuk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAuo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAus">[</span>104<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuw">]</span></span></a></sup> Since the initial construction of the lake, various buildings of national importance have been constructed on its shores.<sup about="#mwt390" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818_105-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTENational Capital Development Commission198818"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818-105"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"National Capital Development Commission"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"18"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818-105" id="mwAu0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAu4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAu8">[</span>105<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAvE"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament_House,_National_Library_of_Australia,_Canberra,_2023.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAvI"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament_House,_National_Library_of_Australia,_Canberra,_2023.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Parliament_House%2C_National_Library_of_Australia%2C_Canberra%2C_2023.jpg/250px-Parliament_House%2C_National_Library_of_Australia%2C_Canberra%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Parliament_House%2C_National_Library_of_Australia%2C_Canberra%2C_2023.jpg/500px-Parliament_House%2C_National_Library_of_Australia%2C_Canberra%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4032" data-file-height="3024" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAvM"/></a><figcaption id="mwAvQ">Various civic landmarks line <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Burley_Griffin" title="Lake Burley Griffin" id="mwAvU">Lake Burley Griffin</a>. Pictured is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Library_of_Australia" title="National Library of Australia" id="mwAvY">National Library</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_House,_Canberra" title="Parliament House, Canberra" id="mwAvc">Parliament House</a> (background)</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAvg">The newly built <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_National_University" title="Australian National University" id="mwAvk">Australian National University</a> was expanded and sculptures as well as monuments were built.<sup about="#mwt393" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818_105-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTENational Capital Development Commission198818"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818-105"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"National Capital Development Commission"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"18"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Capital_Development_Commission198818-105" id="mwAvo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvw">[</span>105<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAv0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt396" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988173–174_106-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988173–174"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988173–174-106"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"173-174"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988173–174-106" id="mwAv4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAv8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwA">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwE">]</span></span></a></sup> A new <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Library_of_Australia" title="National Library of Australia" id="mwAwI">National Library</a> was constructed within the Parliamentary Triangle, followed by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Court_of_Australia" title="High Court of Australia" id="mwAwM">High Court</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Gallery_of_Australia" title="National Gallery of Australia" id="mwAwQ">National Gallery</a>.<sup about="#mwt399" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988116_47-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988116"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988116-47"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"116"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988116-47" id="mwAwU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwc">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt402" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138_107-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138-107"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"138"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138-107" id="mwAwk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAws">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAww">]</span></span></a></sup> Suburbs in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Central" title="Canberra Central" id="mwAw0">Canberra Central</a> (often referred to as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Canberra" title="North Canberra" id="mwAw4">North Canberra</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Canberra" title="South Canberra" id="mwAw8">South Canberra</a>) were further developed in the<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAxA"> </span>1950s and urban development in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suburbs_of_Canberra#Districts" title="Suburbs of Canberra" id="mwAxE">districts</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woden_Valley" title="Woden Valley" id="mwAxI">Woden Valley</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belconnen" title="Belconnen" id="mwAxM">Belconnen</a> commenced in the mid and late 1960s respectively, followed by the district of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuggeranong" title="Tuggeranong" id="mwAxQ">Tuggeranong</a> in the mid-1970s.<sup about="#mwt405" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988250_108-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988250"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988250-108"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"250"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988250-108" id="mwAxU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxc">[</span>108<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt408" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988180_109-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988180"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988180-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"p":{"wt":"180"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988180-109" id="mwAxk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxs">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt416" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-110" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-110"}}'><a href="#cite_note-110" id="mwAx0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAx4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAx8">[</span>110<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyA">]</span></span></a></sup> Many of the new suburbs were named after Australian politicians such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Barton" title="Edmund Barton" id="mwAyE">Barton</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Deakin" title="Alfred Deakin" id="mwAyI">Deakin</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Reid" title="George Reid" id="mwAyM">Reid</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Braddon" title="Edward Braddon" id="mwAyQ">Braddon</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Curtin" title="John Curtin" id="mwAyU">Curtin</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Chifley" title="Ben Chifley" id="mwAyY">Chifley</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Parkes" title="Henry Parkes" id="mwAyc">Parkes</a>.<sup about="#mwt411" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEUniversal_Publishers20076_111-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEUniversal Publishers20076"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEUniversal_Publishers20076-111"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Universal Publishers"},"2":{"wt":"2007"},"p":{"wt":"6"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEUniversal_Publishers20076-111" id="mwAyg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyo">[</span>111<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAys">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAyw">On 9 May 1988, a larger and permanent Parliament House was opened on Capital Hill as part of Australia's bicentenary celebrations.<sup about="#mwt420" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pho_112-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"pho"}}'><a href="#cite_note-pho-112" id="mwAy0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAy4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAy8">[</span>112<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt417" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138_107-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138-107"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Fitzgerald"},"2":{"wt":"1987"},"p":{"wt":"138"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFitzgerald1987138-107" id="mwAzE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzM">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The Commonwealth Parliament moved there from the Provisional Parliament House, now known as Old Parliament House.<sup about="#mwt423" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pho_112-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"pho"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-pho-112"}}'><a href="#cite_note-pho-112" id="mwAzU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzc">[</span>112<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwAzk" aria-labelledby="Self-government"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Self-government">Self-government</h3></div>
<p id="mwAzo">In December 1988, the Australian Capital Territory was granted full self-government by the Commonwealth Parliament, a step proposed as early as 1965.<sup about="#mwt426" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-113" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-113"}}'><a href="#cite_note-113" id="mwAzs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAz0">[</span>113<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAz4">]</span></span></a></sup> Following the first election on 4 March 1989,<sup about="#mwt429" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-114" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-114"}}'><a href="#cite_note-114" id="mwAz8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0E">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0I">]</span></span></a></sup> a 17-member <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Capital_Territory_Legislative_Assembly" title="Australian Capital Territory Legislative Assembly" id="mwA0M">Legislative Assembly</a> sat at temporary offices at 1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwA0Q"> </span>Constitution Avenue, Civic, on 11 May 1989.<sup about="#mwt432" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-115" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-115"}}'><a href="#cite_note-115" id="mwA0U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0c">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt435" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ass_116-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ass"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ass-116"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ass-116" id="mwA0k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0s">[</span>116<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0w">]</span></span></a></sup> Permanent premises were opened on London Circuit in 1994.<sup about="#mwt436" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ass_116-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ass"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ass-116" id="mwA00"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA04"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA08">[</span>116<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1A">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Labor_Party" title="Australian Labor Party" id="mwA1E">Australian Labor Party</a> formed the ACT's first government, led by the Chief Minister <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosemary_Follett" title="Rosemary Follett" id="mwA1I">Rosemary Follett</a>, who made history as Australia's first female head of government.<sup about="#mwt437" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-state_el_117-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"state_el"}}'><a href="#cite_note-state_el-117" id="mwA1M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1U">[</span>117<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1Y">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt440" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-118" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-118"}}'><a href="#cite_note-118" id="mwA1c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1k">[</span>118<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1o">]</span></span></a></sup> The 1990s also saw urban development begin in the district of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gungahlin" title="Gungahlin" id="mwA1s">Gungahlin</a> in the far north of the ACT.<sup about="#mwt443" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-119" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-119"}}'><a href="#cite_note-119" id="mwA1w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA10"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA14">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA18">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt446" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-120" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-120"}}'><a href="#cite_note-120" id="mwA2A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2I">[</span>120<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2M">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA2Q"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA2U"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg/250px-The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg/500px-The_Skywhale_and_Skywhalepapa_February_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2735" data-file-height="2046" data-file-type="bitmap" height="187" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA2Y"/></a><figcaption id="mwA2c">The Skywhale and Skywhalepapa in 2021</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwA2g">Parts of Canberra were engulfed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2003_Canberra_bushfires" title="2003 Canberra bushfires" id="mwA2k">bushfires on 18 January 2003</a> that killed four people, injured 435 and destroyed more than 500 homes as well as the major research telescopes of Australian National University's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Stromlo_Observatory" title="Mount Stromlo Observatory" id="mwA2o">Mount Stromlo Observatory</a>.<sup about="#mwt449" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Doogan_121-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Doogan"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Doogan-121"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Doogan-121" id="mwA2s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA20">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA24">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA28">Throughout 2013, several events celebrated the 100th anniversary of the naming of Canberra.<sup about="#mwt452" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-122" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-122"}}'><a href="#cite_note-122" id="mwA3A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3I">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3M">]</span></span></a></sup> On 11 March 2014, the last day of the centennial year, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Centenary_Column" title="Canberra Centenary Column" id="mwA3Q">Canberra Centenary Column</a> was unveiled in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Hill" title="City Hill" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA3U">City Hill</a>. Other works included <i id="mwA3Y"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Skywhale" title="The Skywhale" id="mwA3c">The Skywhale</a></i>, a hot air balloon designed by the sculptor <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patricia_Piccinini" title="Patricia Piccinini" id="mwA3g">Patricia Piccinini</a>,<sup about="#mwt455" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-123" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-123"}}'><a href="#cite_note-123" id="mwA3k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3s">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3w">]</span></span></a></sup> and <i id="mwA30">StellrScope</i> by visual media artist Eleanor Gates-Stuart.<sup about="#mwt458" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-124" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-124"}}'><a href="#cite_note-124" id="mwA34"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA38"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4A">[</span>124<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4E">]</span></span></a></sup> On 7 February 2021, <i id="mwA4I">The Skywhale</i> was joined by <i id="mwA4M">Skywhalepapa</i> to create a <i id="mwA4Q">Skywhale family</i>, an event marked by Skywhale-themed pastries and beer produced by local companies as well as an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_pop" title="Art pop" id="mwA4U">art pop</a> song entitled "We are the Skywhales".<sup about="#mwt461" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ABC_2021_New_Skywhale_hot_air_balloon_125-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ABC 2021 New Skywhale hot air balloon"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ABC_2021_New_Skywhale_hot_air_balloon-125"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ABC_2021_New_Skywhale_hot_air_balloon-125" id="mwA4Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4g">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA4o">In 2014, Canberra was named the best city to live in the world by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OECD" title="OECD" id="mwA4s">Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development</a>,<sup about="#mwt464" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:3_126-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:3-126"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:3-126" id="mwA4w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA40"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA44">[</span>126<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA48">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt467" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-127" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-127"}}'><a href="#cite_note-127" id="mwA5A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5I">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5M">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt470" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-128" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-128"}}'><a href="#cite_note-128" id="mwA5Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5Y">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5c">]</span></span></a></sup> and was named the third best city to visit in the world by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lonely_Planet" title="Lonely Planet" id="mwA5g">Lonely Planet</a> in 2017.<sup about="#mwt473" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:4_129-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":4"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:4-129"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:4-129" id="mwA5k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5s">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt476" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:5_130-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":5"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:5-130"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:5-130" id="mwA50"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA54"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA58">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwA6E" aria-labelledby="Geography"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Geography">Geography</h2></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA6I"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA6M"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg/250px-Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg/500px-Canberra_SPOT_1088.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6000" data-file-height="6000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="250" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA6Q"/></a><figcaption id="mwA6U">The Canberra region seen from space</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwA6Y">Canberra is located near the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brindabella_Ranges" title="Brindabella Ranges" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA6c">Brindabella Ranges</a> (part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Alps" title="Australian Alps" id="mwA6g">Australian Alps</a>), approximately <span about="#mwt477" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA6k" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"150"},"2":{"wt":"km"}},"i":0}}]}'>150</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt477"> </span><span about="#mwt477">km (93</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt477"> </span><span about="#mwt477">mi)</span> inland from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Coast_of_Australia" title="East Coast of Australia" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA6o">Australia's east coast</a>. Canberra is not incorporated, but its urban environs, which include a large part of the ACT east of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murrumbidgee_River" title="Murrumbidgee River" id="mwA6s">Murrumbidgee River</a>, have an elevation of approximately <span about="#mwt478" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA6w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"580"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"4":{"wt":"-1"}},"i":0}}]}'>580</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt478"> </span><span about="#mwt478">m (1,900</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt478"> </span><span about="#mwt478">ft)</span> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Height_Datum" title="Australian Height Datum" id="mwA60">AHD</a>;<sup about="#mwt489" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bom-131"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwA64"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA68"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7A">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7E">]</span></span></a></sup> the highest point near the built up area is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Majura" title="Mount Majura" id="mwA7I">Mount Majura</a> at <span about="#mwt479" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA7M" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"888"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>888</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt479"> </span><span about="#mwt479">m (2,913</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt479"> </span><span about="#mwt479">ft)</span>.<sup about="#mwt492" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-132" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href="#cite_note-132" id="mwA7Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7Y">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7c">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt495" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-133" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-133"}}'><a href="#cite_note-133" id="mwA7g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7o">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7s">]</span></span></a></sup> Other low mountains include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Taylor_(Australian_Capital_Territory)" title="Mount Taylor (Australian Capital Territory)" id="mwA7w">Mount Taylor</a> <span about="#mwt480" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA70" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"855"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>855</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt480"> </span><span about="#mwt480">m (2,805</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt480"> </span><span about="#mwt480">ft)</span>,<sup about="#mwt498" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-134" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href="#cite_note-134" id="mwA74"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA78"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8A">[</span>134<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8E">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Ainslie" title="Mount Ainslie" id="mwA8I">Mount Ainslie</a> <span about="#mwt481" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA8M" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"843"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>843</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt481"> </span><span about="#mwt481">m (2,766</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt481"> </span><span about="#mwt481">ft)</span>,<sup about="#mwt501" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-135" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-135"}}'><a href="#cite_note-135" id="mwA8Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8Y">[</span>135<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8c">]</span></span></a></sup> Mount Mugga Mugga <span about="#mwt482" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA8g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"812"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>812</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt482"> </span><span about="#mwt482">m (2,664</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt482"> </span><span about="#mwt482">ft)</span><sup about="#mwt504" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-136" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href="#cite_note-136" id="mwA8k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8s">[</span>136<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8w">]</span></span></a></sup> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Mountain_(Australian_Capital_Territory)" title="Black Mountain (Australian Capital Territory)" id="mwA80">Black Mountain</a> <span about="#mwt483" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA84" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"812"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>812</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt483"> </span><span about="#mwt483">m (2,664</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt483"> </span><span about="#mwt483">ft)</span>.<sup about="#mwt507" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-137" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href="#cite_note-137" id="mwA88"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9E">[</span>137<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt484" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEPenguin_Books_Australia200028_138-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEPenguin Books Australia200028"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEPenguin_Books_Australia200028-138"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Penguin Books Australia"},"2":{"wt":"2000"},"p":{"wt":"28"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPenguin_Books_Australia200028-138" id="mwA9M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9U">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA9c">The native forest in the Canberra region was almost wholly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eucalyptus" title="Eucalyptus" id="mwA9g">eucalypt</a> species and provided a resource for fuel and domestic purposes. By the early 1960s, logging had depleted the eucalypt, and concern about water quality led to the forests being closed. Interest in forestry began in 1915 with trials of a number of species including <i id="mwA9k"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus_radiata" title="Pinus radiata" id="mwA9o">Pinus radiata</a></i> on the slopes of Mount Stromlo. Since then, plantations have been expanded, with the benefit of reducing erosion in the Cotter catchment, and the forests are also popular recreation areas.<sup about="#mwt510" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-139" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href="#cite_note-139" id="mwA9s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA90">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA94">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA98"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_Map-MJC.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA-A"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_Map-MJC.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Canberra_Map-MJC.png/250px-Canberra_Map-MJC.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Canberra_Map-MJC.png/500px-Canberra_Map-MJC.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="800" data-file-height="804" data-file-type="bitmap" height="251" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA-E"/></a><figcaption id="mwA-I">The location of Canberra within the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Capital_Territory" title="Australian Capital Territory" id="mwA-M">ACT</a>. Canberra's main districts are shown in yellow: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra_Central" title="Canberra Central" id="mwA-Q">Canberra Central</a> (marked as North Canberra and South Canberra), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woden_Valley" title="Woden Valley" id="mwA-U">Woden Valley</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belconnen" title="Belconnen" id="mwA-Y">Belconnen</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weston_Creek" title="Weston Creek" id="mwA-c">Weston Creek</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuggeranong" title="Tuggeranong" id="mwA-g">Tuggeranong</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gungahlin" title="Gungahlin" id="mwA-k">Gungahlin</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwA-o">The urban environs of Canberra straddle the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginninderra" title="Ginninderra" id="mwA-s">Ginninderra plain</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molonglo_plain" title="Molonglo plain" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA-w">Molonglo plain</a>, the Limestone plain, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuggeranong" title="Tuggeranong" id="mwA-0">Tuggeranong plain</a> (Isabella's Plain).<sup about="#mwt511" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover_140-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"loc":{"wt":"inside cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140" id="mwA-4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_A">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_E">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molonglo_River" title="Molonglo River" id="mwA_I">Molonglo River</a> which flows across the Molonglo plain has been dammed to form the national capital's iconic feature <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Burley_Griffin" title="Lake Burley Griffin" id="mwA_M">Lake Burley Griffin</a>.<sup about="#mwt514" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988131–132_141-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988131–132"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988131–132-141"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"131-132"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988131–132-141" id="mwA_Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_Y">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_c">]</span></span></a></sup> The Molonglo then flows into the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murrumbidgee_River" title="Murrumbidgee River" id="mwA_g">Murrumbidgee</a> north-west of Canberra, which in turn flows north-west toward the New South Wales town of Yass. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queanbeyan_River" title="Queanbeyan River" id="mwA_k">Queanbeyan River</a> joins the Molonglo River at Oaks Estate just within the ACT.<sup about="#mwt517" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover_140-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"loc":{"wt":"inside cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140" id="mwA_o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_w">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA_4">A number of creeks, including Jerrabomberra and Yarralumla Creeks, flow into the Molonglo and Murrumbidgee.<sup about="#mwt520" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover_140-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside cover"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gibbney"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"loc":{"wt":"inside cover"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGibbney1988inside_cover-140" id="mwA_8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAE">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAI">]</span></span></a></sup> Two of these creeks, the Ginninderra and Tuggeranong, have similarly been dammed to form Lakes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Ginninderra" title="Lake Ginninderra" id="mwBAM">Ginninderra</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Tuggeranong" title="Lake Tuggeranong" id="mwBAQ">Tuggeranong</a>.<sup about="#mwt523" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke1988181–182_142-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke1988181–182"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988181–182-142"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"181-182"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke1988181–182-142" id="mwBAU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAc">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt534" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-143" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-143"}}'><a href="#cite_note-143" id="mwBAk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAs">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt526" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilliams2006260_144-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWilliams2006260"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams2006260-144"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Williams"},"2":{"wt":"2006"},"p":{"wt":"260"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams2006260-144" id="mwBA0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBA4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA8">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBA">]</span></span></a></sup> Until recently the Molonglo River had a history of sometimes calamitous floods; the area was a flood plain prior to the filling of Lake Burley Griffin.<sup about="#mwt529" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESparke19884–7,_13–14_145-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESparke19884–7, 13–14"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19884–7,_13–14-145"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sparke"},"2":{"wt":"1988"},"pp":{"wt":"4-7, 13-14"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESparke19884–7,_13–14-145" id="mwBBE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBM">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt537" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NCA_Scrivener_146-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NCA_ Scrivener"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NCA_Scrivener-146"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NCA_Scrivener-146" id="mwBBU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBc">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwBBk" aria-labelledby="Climate"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Climate">Climate</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBBo"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBBs"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg/330px-Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg/960px-Autumn_in_Canberra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="5300" data-file-height="2650" data-file-type="bitmap" height="135" width="270" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBBw"/></a><figcaption id="mwBB0"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autumn_foliage" title="Autumn foliage" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBB4">Autumn foliage</a> in Canberra</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBB8">Under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Köppen_climate_classification" title="Köppen climate classification" id="mwBCA">Köppen-Geiger classification</a>, Canberra has an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oceanic_climate" title="Oceanic climate" id="mwBCE">oceanic climate</a> (<i id="mwBCI">Cfb</i>).<sup about="#mwt542" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-147" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-147"}}'><a href="#cite_note-147" id="mwBCM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCU">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCY">]</span></span></a></sup> In January, the warmest month, the average high is approximately <span about="#mwt538" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBCc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"29"},"2":{"wt":"C"},"3":{"wt":"F"}},"i":0}}]}'>29</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt538"> </span><span about="#mwt538">°C (84</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt538"> </span><span about="#mwt538">°F)</span>; in July, the coldest month, the average high drops to approximately <span about="#mwt539" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBCg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"12"},"2":{"wt":"C"},"3":{"wt":"F"}},"i":0}}]}'>12</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt539"> </span><span about="#mwt539">°C (54</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt539"> </span><span about="#mwt539">°F)</span>.</p>

<p id="mwBCk">Frost is common in the winter months. Snow is rare in the CBD (central business district) due to being on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leeward" title="Leeward" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBCo">leeward</a> (eastern) side of the dividing range, but the surrounding areas get annual snowfall through winter and often the snow-capped <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brindabella_Range" title="Brindabella Range" id="mwBCs">Brindabella Range</a> can be seen from the CBD. The last significant snowfall in the city centre was in 1968.<sup about="#mwt543" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBCw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBC0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC4">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC8">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBDA">The highest recorded maximum temperature was <span about="#mwt544" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBDE" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"44.0"},"2":{"wt":"°C"}},"i":0}}]}'>44.0</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt544"> </span><span about="#mwt544">°C (111.2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt544"> </span><span about="#mwt544">°F)</span> on 4 January 2020.<sup about="#mwt549" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-148" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-148"}}'><a href="#cite_note-148" id="mwBDI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDQ">[</span>148<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDU">]</span></span></a></sup> Winter 2011 was Canberra's warmest winter on record, approximately <span about="#mwt545" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBDY" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"},"3":{"wt":"0"}},"i":0}}]}'>2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt545"> </span><span about="#mwt545">°C (4</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt545"> </span><span about="#mwt545">°F)</span> above the average temperature.<sup about="#mwt552" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-149" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-149"}}'><a href="#cite_note-149" id="mwBDc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDk">[</span>149<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDo">]</span></span></a></sup> The lowest recorded minimum temperature was <span about="#mwt546" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBDs" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"−10.0"},"2":{"wt":"°C"}},"i":0}}]}'>−10.0</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt546"> </span><span about="#mwt546">°C (14.0</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt546"> </span><span about="#mwt546">°F)</span> on the morning of 11 July 1971.<sup about="#mwt553" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBDw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBD0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD4">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD8">]</span></span></a></sup> Light snow falls only once in every few years, and is usually not widespread and quickly dissipates.<sup about="#mwt554" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBEA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEI">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBEQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_warming.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBEU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_warming.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Canberra_warming.jpg/250px-Canberra_warming.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Canberra_warming.jpg/500px-Canberra_warming.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="896" data-file-height="451" data-file-type="bitmap" height="126" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBEY"/></a><figcaption id="mwBEc">Long-term temperature increase in Canberra</figcaption></figure> 
<p id="mwBEg">Canberra is protected from the west by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brindabellas" title="Brindabellas" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBEk">Brindabellas</a> which create a strong <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rain_shadow" title="Rain shadow" id="mwBEo">rain shadow</a> in Canberra's valleys.<sup about="#mwt555" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBEs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBE0">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBE4">]</span></span></a></sup> Canberra gets 100.4 clear days annually.<sup about="#mwt558" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-autogenerated1_150-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"autogenerated1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-autogenerated1-150"}}'><a href="#cite_note-autogenerated1-150" id="mwBE8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFE">[</span>150<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFI">]</span></span></a></sup> Annual rainfall is the third lowest of the capital cities (after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adelaide" title="Adelaide" id="mwBFM">Adelaide</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hobart" title="Hobart" id="mwBFQ">Hobart</a>)<sup about="#mwt562" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-151" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-151"}}'><a href="#cite_note-151" id="mwBFU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFc">[</span>151<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFg">]</span></span></a></sup> and is spread fairly evenly over the seasons, with late spring bringing the highest rainfall.<sup about="#mwt565" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-152" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-152"}}'><a href="#cite_note-152" id="mwBFk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFs">[</span>152<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFw">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunderstorm" title="Thunderstorm" id="mwBF0">Thunderstorms</a> occur mostly between October and April, owing to the effect of summer and the mountains.<sup about="#mwt566" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBF4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBF8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGA">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGE">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBGI">The area is generally <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Australian_foehn" title="Southeast Australian foehn" id="mwBGM">sheltered from a westerly wind</a>, though strong northwesterlies can develop. A cool, vigorous afternoon easterly change, colloquially referred to as a 'sea-breeze' or the 'Braidwood Butcher',<sup about="#mwt569" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-153" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-153"}}'><a href="#cite_note-153" id="mwBGQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGY">[</span>153<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt572" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-154" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-154"}}'><a href="#cite_note-154" id="mwBGg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGo">[</span>154<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGs">]</span></span></a></sup> is common during the summer months<sup about="#mwt575" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-155" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href="#cite_note-155" id="mwBGw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBG0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBG4">[</span>155<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBG8">]</span></span></a></sup> and often exceeds 40<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBHA"> </span>km/h in the city. Canberra is also less humid than the nearby coastal areas.<sup about="#mwt576" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bom_131-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bom"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bom-131" id="mwBHE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHM">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBHU">Canberra was severely affected by smoke haze during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019–20_Australian_bushfire_season" title="2019–20 Australian bushfire season" id="mwBHY">2019/2020 bushfires</a>. On 1 January 2020, Canberra had the worst air quality of any major city in the world, with an AQI of 7700 (USAQI 949).<sup about="#mwt579" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-156" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-156"}}'><a href="#cite_note-156" id="mwBHc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHk">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
